#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "db20f4a5-27a4-5fbc-aae2-2febf8f99cb1" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e57f9dab-c5db-55d9-9b21-375bbc92281c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6814" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/e4dfe9db8587b4dfa4fae9c4610106a3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n$x= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e75170b-1e28-52c1-b0ab-f50ffde6c262" :knowledge-point/title "The Cartesian Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "8f1ee01d-29ac-5fb8-a5c9-e0995afa269c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6815" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{4} &= \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4^{2}} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y + 1 & = 2 \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{y + 1}{2} & = \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} + \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  = 1 .\\end{array}$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151918 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d82541c-40ca-5df9-aa17-c20b201fadd4" :knowledge-point/title "The Parametric Equations of an Ellipse Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d97dc879-9840-5531-8247-5229e5eb8bb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6816" :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/ad11664b2d980183e25e80a1d5c231cd.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$x= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k)$.\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2)$, $a=6$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151919 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "9abded65-4a6e-5ebc-b496-d61b28179288" :question/math-academy-id "e-6818" :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/83eed01dabcfcec70e64963213c46397.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n$x= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n$x= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/86476fd8ded16a035b000e1865623698.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to\n\n$$\n\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b51a7bc-7701-539f-9e20-dfc7f2574559" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b948497c-ba12-5f95-a24a-1474982b4aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151219} {:answer/id #uuid "e47ccafa-6225-5d11-b961-b021c8d8596c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151220} {:answer/id #uuid "11a79453-e9b4-5b45-8936-447fd601330c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\pi , \\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151221} {:answer/id #uuid "741d1f75-acfd-5548-871d-d5776ee66bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [- \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151222} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7f7153-c6f5-50b7-90e5-33e174228d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e47ccafa-6225-5d11-b961-b021c8d8596c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta  \\in  [0, \\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151220} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b74498a-4928-5919-b099-ecc7b262886f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151218}] :question/id #uuid "158a895e-0de7-58b3-a9d1-1bc720ae5d4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-78682" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-78682.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?"}]}
